var e=[{topicId:`pe-fluid-flow`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Fluid flow is the unit operation of moving liquids and gases through pipes, pumps and fittings. In a pharmaceutical plant it governs how syrup is transferred from a mixing vessel to a filling line, how purified water is circulated, and how a filter or heat exchanger is sized.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Words you must be able to define`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Fluid: any substance that deforms continuously under a shearing force, so both liquids and gases are fluids.`,`Viscosity (mu): the internal resistance of a fluid to flow. SI unit pascal second (Pa s); the CGS unit is poise, and 1 Pa s = 10 poise.`,`Kinematic viscosity (nu): viscosity divided by density, nu = mu / rho, unit square metre per second. The CGS unit is the stoke.`,`Ideal fluid: an imaginary fluid that is incompressible and has zero viscosity. Real fluids always have viscosity.`,`Velocity (v): distance travelled by the fluid per unit time, metre per second. Average velocity = volumetric flow rate / cross-sectional area.`,`Newtonian fluid: viscosity stays constant whatever the shear rate, for example water, glycerin and light liquid paraffin.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Types of flow`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Laminar (streamline or viscous) flow: the fluid moves in parallel layers that slide over one another and do not mix. Velocity is highest at the centre of the pipe and zero at the wall, giving a parabolic velocity profile.`,`Turbulent flow: the fluid moves in a chaotic way with eddies and cross-currents. The velocity profile is flat over most of the pipe and mixing is intense.`,`Transitional flow: the unstable region between the two, where flow flips between streamline and turbulent.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`Osborne Reynolds showed with a dye-filament experiment that the type of flow can be predicted from a single dimensionless number. A fine thread of dye stays as a straight line in laminar flow and disperses completely in turbulent flow.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Re = D v rho / mu`,caption:`D = internal pipe diameter (m), v = average linear velocity (m/s), rho = density (kg/m3), mu = viscosity (Pa s). Re is dimensionless.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Reynolds number (pipes)`,`Type of flow`,`Behaviour of dye thread`],rows:[[`Less than 2000`,`Laminar / streamline`,`Stays as a straight thread`],[`2000 to 4000`,`Transitional`,`Thread wavers and breaks up`],[`More than 4000`,`Turbulent`,`Disperses across the whole pipe`]],caption:`These ranges apply to flow inside circular pipes only. Other geometries have different critical values.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Re is dimensionless`,text:`Reynolds number has no units, because it is the ratio of inertial force to viscous force. Being asked for the units of Re is a standard trick; the answer is that it has none.`}]},{topicId:`pe-fluid-flow`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At exam level you apply the energy balance (Bernoulli equation), pick the right flow meter for a stated duty, and read a manometer. Most GPAT engineering questions on this topic are one-step substitutions into these equations.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Bernoulli equation`},{kind:`para`,text:`For an ideal incompressible fluid in steady flow, the total mechanical energy at any point along a streamline is constant. Written as heads, in metres of the flowing fluid:`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`P/(rho g) + v^2/(2g) + Z = constant`,caption:`P/(rho g) = pressure head, v^2/(2g) = velocity (kinetic) head, Z = potential (static) head above the datum, g = 9.81 m/s2.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Assumptions: steady flow, incompressible fluid, no friction, no work added or removed, flow along a single streamline.`,`For a real fluid the equation is corrected by adding a friction head loss term F and any pump work W: P1/(rho g) + v1^2/(2g) + Z1 + W = P2/(rho g) + v2^2/(2g) + Z2 + F.`,`Consequence: where a pipe narrows the velocity rises, so the velocity head rises and the pressure head must fall. Every variable-head flow meter is built on exactly this consequence.`,`The same principle explains the working of a carburettor, an atomiser and a venturi scrubber.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Continuity equation`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`A1 v1 = A2 v2 = Q`,caption:`A = cross-sectional area (m2), v = average velocity (m/s), Q = volumetric flow rate (m3/s). It is a statement of conservation of mass for an incompressible fluid.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Flow measurement devices`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Device`,`Principle`,`What it measures`,`Permanent pressure loss`],rows:[[`Orifice meter`,`Variable head, thin plate with a hole`,`Average velocity / flow rate`,`High`],[`Venturi meter`,`Variable head, gradual converge-diverge`,`Average velocity / flow rate`,`Low (about 10 percent)`],[`Pitot tube`,`Impact (stagnation) versus static pressure`,`Point velocity at one place only`,`Very low`],[`Rotameter`,`Variable area, constant pressure drop`,`Flow rate read directly off a scale`,`Low and constant`]],caption:`Orifice, venturi and pitot tube are variable-head meters; the rotameter is a variable-area meter.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Orifice meter: cheapest, needs least space, easy to install between flanges, but the sudden contraction and expansion cause a large permanent loss of pressure and it clogs with suspended solids.`,`Venturi meter: a converging cone, a short throat and a long diverging cone (the recovery cone). The gradual taper prevents eddies, so most of the pressure is recovered. Expensive, bulky, but preferred where pumping cost matters.`,`Pitot tube: two concentric tubes; the inner opening faces the flow and senses impact pressure, the outer holes sense static pressure. It gives the velocity at one point, so the pipe must be traversed to obtain the average.`,`Rotameter: a vertical tapered glass tube with a float. As flow rises the float rises until the annular area gives a drag force that balances its weight, so the pressure drop across the float stays constant while the area varies. It is a direct-reading meter and needs no manometer.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Q = Cd x A0 x sqrt(2 g H)  (approximate form when the approach velocity is negligible)`,caption:`Cd = coefficient of discharge, A0 = area of the throat or orifice (m2), H = differential head expressed in metres of the flowing fluid.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`v = Cv x sqrt(2 g h)`,caption:`Pitot tube point velocity. h = difference between impact and static head in metres of flowing fluid; Cv is close to 1 for a well-made tube.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Manometers`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Simple manometer: a vertical or U-tube containing a heavy immiscible liquid (usually mercury); one limb is open to the atmosphere and the other is connected to the pipe. Reads gauge pressure at one point.`,`Differential (U-tube) manometer: both limbs are connected to two points, so it reads the pressure difference directly. This is the standard partner of an orifice or venturi meter.`,`Inclined manometer: the limb is tilted so a small vertical rise becomes a long reading along the tube, magnifying sensitivity for small pressure differences (used for gas flow).`,`Two-liquid (differential) manometer: uses two immiscible liquids of nearly equal density in an enlarged-limb design, which magnifies the reading further for very small differences.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`delta P = (rho_m - rho_f) x g x R`,caption:`rho_m = density of manometric (heavy) liquid, rho_f = density of the flowing fluid, R = manometer reading in metres, g = 9.81 m/s2.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Pitot tube versus orifice meter`,text:`A pitot tube gives the velocity at a single point, not the flow through the pipe; an orifice or venturi gives the average velocity over the whole cross-section. Choosing "pitot tube measures rate of flow" is the standard wrong answer.`}]},{topicId:`pe-fluid-flow`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced material is quantitative: friction losses, the laminar-flow equations that can be derived from first principles, and the Reynolds numbers used outside straight pipes.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Friction in pipes`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`F = 4 f L v^2 / (2 g D)`,caption:`Fanning equation for frictional head loss in metres. f = Fanning friction factor, L = pipe length (m), D = diameter (m), v = velocity (m/s).`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`In laminar flow the Fanning friction factor is a pure function of Reynolds number: f = 16 / Re. Roughness has no effect.`,`If the Darcy (Moody) friction factor is used instead, its laminar value is 64 / Re, that is four times the Fanning value. Always check which factor a question means.`,`In turbulent flow f depends on both Re and relative roughness (e/D) and is read from a Moody chart.`,`Losses at fittings are handled as an equivalent length of straight pipe, or as K x v^2 / 2g where K is a fitting constant.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Q = pi x delta P x r^4 / (8 mu L)`,caption:`Hagen-Poiseuille equation for laminar flow of a Newtonian fluid in a circular tube. Note the fourth power of radius: halving the radius cuts flow to one sixteenth.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`In fully developed laminar flow the average velocity is exactly half the maximum (centre-line) velocity: v_avg = 0.5 v_max.`,`In turbulent flow the profile is much flatter and v_avg is roughly 0.8 times v_max.`,`Critical velocity is the velocity at which flow changes character; from Re = 2000, v_critical = 2000 mu / (D rho).`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Worked numerical`},{kind:`para`,text:`Water (rho 1000 kg/m3, mu 0.001 Pa s) flows at 2 m/s in a 50 mm pipe. Re = (0.05 x 2 x 1000) / 0.001 = 100000, which is far above 4000, so the flow is turbulent. If instead a syrup of viscosity 0.5 Pa s and density 1250 kg/m3 flowed at the same velocity, Re = (0.05 x 2 x 1250) / 0.5 = 250, which is well below 2000, so the same pipe now runs laminar.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Reynolds number outside straight pipes`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Re (stirred vessel) = D^2 N rho / mu`,caption:`D = impeller diameter (m), N = rotational speed (revolutions per second). Laminar below about 10, turbulent above about 10000 for a mixing tank.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`For non-circular ducts use the equivalent (hydraulic) diameter, De = 4 x cross-sectional area / wetted perimeter.`,`Typical discharge coefficients: sharp-edged orifice about 0.6, venturi about 0.98. The venturi is therefore both more accurate and more efficient, and the orifice is chosen only on cost and space.`,`A venturi returns roughly 90 percent of the pressure it borrows; a sharp-edged orifice permanently loses a large fraction of the measured differential, which is why it is avoided on long continuous duties.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Rotameter is the odd one out`,text:`Orifice, venturi and pitot all work at constant area with a varying pressure drop. The rotameter works at constant pressure drop with a varying area. Any question asking for the variable-area meter, or for the meter with a constant pressure drop, is pointing at the rotameter.`}]},{topicId:`pe-heat-evaporation`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Heat transfer is the movement of thermal energy from a hot body to a cold one. Every sterilisation cycle, every drying step and every evaporation of an extract depends on it. Evaporation is the specific unit operation of concentrating a solution by boiling off the solvent, usually water, leaving the solute behind.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The three mechanisms`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Conduction: heat passes from molecule to molecule without any bulk movement of matter. It is the mechanism in solids, for example through the wall of a stainless steel jacketed kettle.`,`Convection: heat is carried by the actual movement of the fluid. Natural convection is driven by density differences created by heating; forced convection is driven by a pump, stirrer or fan.`,`Radiation: heat travels as electromagnetic waves and needs no medium at all, for example an infrared lamp drying a granule bed.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Q = k A (T1 - T2) / x`,caption:`Fourier law of conduction. Q = heat flow (W or J/s), k = thermal conductivity (W/m K), A = area normal to flow (m2), (T1 - T2) = temperature difference (K), x = thickness (m).`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Q = h A (Ts - Tf)`,caption:`Newton's law of cooling for convection. h = film or surface heat transfer coefficient (W/m2 K), Ts = surface temperature, Tf = bulk fluid temperature.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Q = sigma x epsilon x A x T^4`,caption:`Stefan-Boltzmann law for radiation. sigma = 5.67 x 10^-8 W/m2 K4, epsilon = emissivity (1 for a black body), T = absolute temperature in kelvin.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Thermal conductivity k is a property of the material: metals are high (copper about 380, stainless steel about 16 W/m K), while insulators such as asbestos, cork and glass wool are very low.`,`A good conductor makes a good heat exchanger tube; a poor conductor makes good lagging.`,`Latent heat of vaporisation is the heat needed to turn one kilogram of boiling liquid into vapour at the same temperature. For water at 100 degrees C it is about 2257 kJ/kg, and supplying this is the main cost of evaporation.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Which law goes with which mechanism`,text:`Fourier for conduction, Newton for convection, Stefan-Boltzmann for radiation. Remember the order C-C-R matched to F-N-S.`}]},{topicId:`pe-heat-evaporation`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The working exam level joins the film coefficients into one overall coefficient, sizes an exchanger with the log mean temperature difference, and classifies evaporators by how the liquid circulates.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Overall heat transfer coefficient`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`1/U = 1/hi + x/k + 1/ho`,caption:`U = overall heat transfer coefficient (W/m2 K), hi and ho = inside and outside film coefficients, x/k = resistance of the metal wall. Scale and dirt films add further resistance terms.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Resistances in series add, so the largest single resistance controls the rate. In a steam-heated evaporator the liquid-side film and any scale layer, not the metal, are usually controlling.`,`Dropwise condensation of steam gives a much higher coefficient than filmwise condensation, but filmwise is what happens in practice on clean industrial surfaces.`,`Turbulence raises h, which is why forced circulation and stirring speed up heating.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Q = U A (delta T)lm`,caption:`Design equation for an exchanger. (delta T)lm is the log mean temperature difference between the two streams.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Heat exchangers`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type`,`Construction`,`Best for`],rows:[[`Double pipe`,`One pipe inside another`,`Small duties, high pressure`],[`Shell and tube`,`Tube bundle inside a shell, baffled`,`Large industrial duties`],[`Plate (gasketed)`,`Corrugated plates in a frame`,`Clean liquids, easy cleaning, CIP`],[`Coil / jacketed vessel`,`Coil or jacket on a kettle`,`Batch heating of syrups and creams`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Parallel (co-current) flow: both fluids enter at the same end. The outlet temperature of the cold fluid can never exceed that of the hot fluid, and the driving force falls rapidly.`,`Counter-current flow: the fluids enter at opposite ends. The driving force is more uniform, the mean temperature difference is larger, and a smaller area is needed for the same duty. Counter-current is the preferred arrangement.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Evaporator types`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Evaporator`,`Circulation`,`Suited to`],rows:[[`Steam jacketed kettle`,`Natural, open pan`,`Small batches, non heat-sensitive`],[`Short tube (calandria)`,`Natural, via central downtake`,`Low viscosity, non-scaling liquids`],[`Long tube climbing film`,`Natural, once-through thin film`,`Foaming and heat-sensitive liquids`],[`Forced circulation`,`Pump driven through tubes`,`Viscous, scaling or salting liquids`],[`Falling film`,`Liquid fed at top, flows down as film`,`Very heat-sensitive liquids, short contact`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Short tube vertical (Robert or calandria) evaporator: short tubes about 1 to 2 m long set in a steam chest, with a wide central downtake through which cooled liquid returns. Simple and cheap, but poor with viscous liquids.`,`Long tube vertical climbing film evaporator: tubes several metres long; vapour formed near the bottom expands and drags the liquid up the wall as a thin film, giving a very short contact time. Also called the climbing film evaporator.`,`Forced circulation evaporator: a pump gives high velocity in the tubes, which raises the film coefficient and stops solids depositing. It is the choice when the liquid is viscous or deposits scale.`,`Film evaporators avoid keeping the product hot for long, so they suit thermolabile extracts and vitamins.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Evaporation is not distillation or drying`,text:`In evaporation the product wanted is the concentrated liquid and the vapour is discarded. In distillation the vapour is condensed and kept, and it separates two volatile components. In drying the product is a dry solid.`}]},{topicId:`pe-heat-evaporation`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions turn on the two performance measures of an evaporator, on why multiple-effect working saves steam, and on the arithmetic of the design equation.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Capacity and economy`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Capacity = kilograms of water evaporated per hour`,caption:`A measure of throughput; it depends on U, area and temperature difference.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Economy (steam economy) = kg of vapour produced / kg of steam supplied`,caption:`A measure of efficiency of steam use. For a single effect it is a little below 1, typically around 0.8 to 0.9, because some steam heats the feed to its boiling point.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Multiple effect evaporation`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The vapour leaving the first effect is used as the heating medium in the second effect, whose vapour heats the third, and so on. Each effect works at a lower pressure, so its liquid boils at a lower temperature and the vapour from the previous effect is hot enough to boil it.`,`One kilogram of live steam therefore evaporates roughly one kilogram of water in each effect, so economy rises approximately in proportion to the number of effects while steam consumption falls.`,`Capacity, however, does not increase. The total available temperature drop is shared between the effects, so each effect has a smaller driving force; an n-effect unit has about the same total capacity as a single effect of the same total area, but uses about 1/n of the steam.`,`The practical limit on the number of effects is set by boiling point elevation, capital cost and the smallest workable temperature difference per effect.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feeding method`,`Path of liquid`,`Comment`],rows:[[`Forward feed`,`Feed and steam both enter effect 1`,`Simple, no pumps; viscous liquid meets coolest effect`],[`Backward feed`,`Feed enters the last (coolest) effect`,`Better for viscous liquids; needs pumps`],[`Mixed feed`,`Combination of the two`,`Compromise, avoids some pumping`],[`Parallel feed`,`Fresh feed to every effect`,`Used for crystallising solutions`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Worked numericals`},{kind:`para`,text:`Conduction: a stainless steel wall 10 mm thick with k = 16 W/m K and area 2 m2 has 120 degrees C on one side and 100 degrees C on the other. Q = 16 x 2 x 20 / 0.01 = 64000 W = 64 kW.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Overall coefficient: if hi = 1000 and ho = 4000 W/m2 K and the wall resistance is negligible, 1/U = 1/1000 + 1/4000 = 0.00125, so U = 800 W/m2 K. Notice the answer is dominated by the smaller coefficient.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`(delta T)lm = (delta T1 - delta T2) / ln(delta T1 / delta T2)`,caption:`Log mean temperature difference. If the terminal differences are 50 K and 20 K, (delta T)lm = 30 / ln 2.5 = 30 / 0.916 = 32.7 K.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Economy rises, capacity does not`,text:`The single most repeated statement on evaporation is that multiple effect evaporation increases steam economy without increasing capacity. Options that claim capacity is multiplied by the number of effects are wrong.`}]},{topicId:`pe-drying-humidity`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Drying is the removal of small amounts of liquid, usually water, from a solid by evaporation into a gas stream. Because the drying medium is nearly always air, you must first be able to describe how much water air is already carrying. That description is called psychrometry or humidity.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Psychrometric terms`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Absolute humidity (humidity, H): the mass of water vapour carried by unit mass of dry air, in kg water per kg dry air. It does not change when the air is simply heated.`,`Saturation humidity: the absolute humidity of air that is holding all the water vapour it can at that temperature.`,`Relative humidity (RH): the partial pressure of water vapour in the air expressed as a percentage of the vapour pressure of pure water at the same temperature.`,`Percentage humidity: the actual absolute humidity expressed as a percentage of the saturation humidity at the same temperature.`,`Dew point: the temperature to which the air must be cooled, at constant pressure, before it becomes saturated and water begins to condense.`,`Dry bulb temperature: the ordinary air temperature read by a normal thermometer.`,`Wet bulb temperature: the steady temperature read by a thermometer whose bulb is covered with a wet wick over which air is blown rapidly. Evaporation cools the wick, so it reads lower than the dry bulb.`,`Wet bulb depression: dry bulb temperature minus wet bulb temperature. It is zero in saturated air and large in dry air.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`RH = (p / ps) x 100`,caption:`p = partial pressure of water vapour in the air, ps = saturation vapour pressure of water at the same dry bulb temperature.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Percentage humidity = (H / Hs) x 100`,caption:`H = actual absolute humidity, Hs = saturation humidity at the same temperature.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Moisture in a wet solid`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Equilibrium moisture content: the moisture the solid still holds when it has come to equilibrium with air of a stated temperature and humidity. It cannot be removed by drying under those conditions.`,`Free moisture: the moisture present over and above the equilibrium value. Only free moisture can actually be dried off.`,`Bound moisture: water held in fine capillaries, in cell walls or as a solution, so it exerts a vapour pressure lower than that of pure water at the same temperature. It is hard to remove.`,`Unbound moisture: water held loosely on the surface or in large voids; it exerts the full vapour pressure of pure water and is removed easily.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Free and unbound are not the same thing`,text:`Free moisture is defined against the equilibrium moisture content, so it is a removable quantity. Bound and unbound are defined against the vapour pressure of pure water, so they describe how the water is held. Some free moisture can still be bound moisture.`}]},{topicId:`pe-drying-humidity`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The centre of this topic is the drying rate curve. Plot the rate of drying (kg water removed per unit area per unit time) against the free moisture content of the solid and the whole behaviour of a dryer becomes readable.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The drying rate curve`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Initial adjustment period: the wet solid comes to the steady temperature of the drying air. It is short and often ignored.`,`Constant rate period: the surface stays completely wetted with a continuous film of unbound water. The rate of drying is constant and the surface sits at the wet bulb temperature of the air.`,`Critical moisture content: the point at the end of the constant rate period where the surface film first breaks and dry patches appear.`,`First falling rate period (unsaturated surface drying): the wetted area of the surface shrinks steadily, so the rate falls in an almost straight line.`,`Second falling rate period: the whole surface is dry and the rate is now controlled by diffusion of moisture from inside the solid. Drying ends when the equilibrium moisture content is reached and the rate becomes zero.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Constant rate period`,`Falling rate period`],rows:[[`Moisture removed`,`Unbound surface moisture`,`Bound / internal moisture`],[`Rate controlled by`,`Air conditions outside the solid`,`Movement of water inside the solid`],[`Surface temperature`,`Wet bulb temperature of the air`,`Rises towards the dry bulb`],[`Effect of air velocity`,`Large, raises the rate`,`Little or none`],[`Effect of solid thickness`,`None`,`Large, thicker means slower`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Raising the air temperature, raising the air velocity and lowering the air humidity all increase the constant rate.`,`Case hardening is the formation of a dry impermeable skin when the surface is dried too fast; moisture is then trapped inside. It is avoided by drying more gently at first.`,`Over-drying below the equilibrium moisture content wastes energy and can harm the product, so dryers are stopped at a specified loss on drying.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Dryers and what they suit`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Dryer`,`Mode of heating`,`Material it suits`],rows:[[`Tray (shelf) dryer`,`Convection over static trays`,`Small batches, granules, crude drugs`],[`Drum dryer`,`Conduction from a heated drum`,`Slurries, pastes, thin suspensions`],[`Spray dryer`,`Convection onto atomised droplets`,`Solutions and suspensions, heat-sensitive`],[`Fluidised bed dryer`,`Convection through a suspended bed`,`Granules for tabletting, free-flowing solids`],[`Vacuum dryer`,`Conduction under reduced pressure`,`Thermolabile, hygroscopic, solvent-wet solids`],[`Freeze dryer`,`Sublimation under high vacuum`,`Vaccines, sera, enzymes, blood products`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Spray drying converts a liquid feed straight into a dry free-flowing powder in a few seconds, because the droplet stays near the wet bulb temperature while it dries. It gives spherical, uniform, often hollow particles with good flow.`,`Fluidised bed drying gives a very large surface area per unit volume and near-perfect mixing, so it is far faster than a tray dryer, but attrition of fragile granules and static build-up are its drawbacks.`,`Freeze drying works below the triple point: the material is frozen, then ice is sublimed directly to vapour under vacuum. Product quality is excellent and the cake reconstitutes instantly, but it is the slowest and most expensive method.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Wet bulb temperature in the constant rate period`,text:`During the constant rate period the wet surface stays at the wet bulb temperature of the drying air, because all the heat arriving is used as latent heat of vaporisation. This is asked directly and also as the reason spray drying is safe for thermolabile drugs.`}]},{topicId:`pe-drying-humidity`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced work means calculating humidity from vapour pressures, using the humid heat, and knowing the stages and the rate-limiting steps of freeze drying.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`H = 0.622 x p / (P - p)`,caption:`Absolute humidity of moist air in kg water per kg dry air. 0.622 = 18/29, the ratio of the molar masses of water and air. P = total pressure, p = partial pressure of water vapour, in the same units.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Worked example: air at 760 mm Hg contains water vapour at a partial pressure of 12 mm Hg, and the saturation vapour pressure at that temperature is 24 mm Hg. Relative humidity = 12/24 x 100 = 50 percent. Absolute humidity = 0.622 x 12 / (760 - 12) = 0.622 x 0.01604 = 0.00998, that is about 0.010 kg water per kg dry air.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Humid heat cs = 1.005 + 1.88 H`,caption:`kJ per kg dry air per kelvin. 1.005 is the specific heat of dry air and 1.88 that of water vapour, both in kJ/kg K.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Percentage humidity is always numerically smaller than relative humidity at the same condition, except at 0 and 100 percent where they coincide. This is a favourite one-line question.`,`Heating air at constant pressure leaves absolute humidity unchanged but lowers relative humidity, which is exactly why heated air dries better.`,`The adiabatic saturation temperature and the wet bulb temperature are nearly equal for the air-water system, which is why the psychrometric chart for air-water can use one set of lines for both.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Rc = h (T - Tw) / lambda_w`,caption:`Constant drying rate per unit area. h = heat transfer coefficient, T = dry bulb temperature, Tw = wet bulb temperature, lambda_w = latent heat of vaporisation at Tw.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Freeze drying in detail`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Pre-treatment and freezing: the product is frozen well below its eutectic temperature so that all the water is in the solid state.`,`Primary drying (sublimation): pressure is lowered below the triple point of water and gentle heat is supplied; ice sublimes directly to vapour. This removes the bulk of the water and is the longest stage.`,`Secondary drying (desorption): the temperature is raised to remove the residual bound moisture adsorbed on the solid, bringing the final moisture down to a low percentage.`,`The condenser, held far colder than the product, traps the water vapour and is what actually maintains the low vapour pressure driving sublimation.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Dryer`,`Typical drying time`,`Key limitation`],rows:[[`Spray dryer`,`Seconds`,`High capital cost, low thermal efficiency`],[`Fluidised bed`,`Minutes`,`Attrition, static charge, dust`],[`Tray dryer`,`Hours`,`Slow, uneven, labour intensive`],[`Freeze dryer`,`Many hours to days`,`Very slow and very expensive`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Match the dryer to the feed`,text:`Liquid feed to dry powder in one step means spray drying. Slurry on a heated roll means drum drying. Wet granules before compression means fluidised bed. A sterile biological in a vial means freeze drying. Most dryer questions are simply this match.`}]},{topicId:`pe-size-reduction-separation`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Size reduction, also called comminution or milling, is the mechanical breaking of large solid particles into smaller ones. Size separation, also called classification, then sorts the milled powder into fractions of a chosen size range. The two operations almost always run as a pair.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Why we reduce particle size`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A smaller particle has a larger surface area per unit mass, so it dissolves faster and, for a poorly soluble drug, is absorbed better (griseofulvin is the classical example).`,`Extraction of crude drugs is faster because solvent reaches more cell contents.`,`Powders of similar size mix uniformly, which is essential for content uniformity of a low-dose tablet.`,`Drying is quicker because moisture has a shorter path to the surface.`,`Suspensions and ointments are smoother and physically more stable.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Mechanisms of size reduction`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Cutting: a sharp blade slices the material, used for tough fibrous crude drugs.`,`Compression: the material is crushed between two hard surfaces, used for coarse hard solids.`,`Impact: a fast-moving hammer or ball strikes the particle, used for brittle crystalline materials.`,`Attrition (shear or rubbing): particles are rubbed between surfaces, used for soft and fibrous materials and for very fine grinding.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Factors affecting size reduction`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Hardness: hard materials need impact or compression and cause wear; abrasive materials contaminate the product with metal.`,`Toughness: a tough material absorbs energy without fracturing. Cooling it, for example with liquid nitrogen, makes it brittle and easier to mill.`,`Stickiness and moisture: sticky material clogs the mill; a small amount of moisture may help, but too much causes balling.`,`Softening by heat: waxy and low-melting materials soften during milling, so a cooled mill or a fluid energy mill is used.`,`Physiological effect: potent or irritant drugs must be milled in a closed system such as a ball mill.`,`Purity required: the mill must not shed metal or need cleaning solvents that contaminate the product.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Size reduction is very inefficient`,text:`Only a small fraction of the energy fed to a mill actually creates new surface; most is lost as heat, noise and elastic deformation. This is why heat-sensitive drugs are milled in a fluid energy mill, where the expanding gas cools the material instead of heating it.`}]},{topicId:`pe-size-reduction-separation`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The exam level covers the three energy laws, the mills and what each suits, the critical speed of a ball mill, and the standard methods of separating a milled powder by size.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Laws of size reduction`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`dE/dL = -K x L^(-n)`,caption:`The general differential form. E = energy per unit mass, L = particle size. Putting n = 2 gives Rittinger, n = 1 gives Kick and n = 1.5 gives Bond.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Rittinger: E = Kr x (1/d2 - 1/d1)`,caption:`Energy is proportional to the new surface area produced. d1 = initial size, d2 = final size, Kr = Rittinger constant. Best for fine grinding.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Kick: E = Kk x ln(d1 / d2)`,caption:`Energy depends only on the size reduction ratio d1/d2, not on the absolute sizes. Best for coarse crushing.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Bond: E = 2 Kb x (1/sqrt(d2) - 1/sqrt(d1))`,caption:`Bond law, intermediate between the other two. In the work index form, W = 10 Wi x (1/sqrt(P80) - 1/sqrt(F80)) with sizes in micrometres.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Law`,`Energy proportional to`,`Applies best to`],rows:[[`Rittinger`,`New surface area created`,`Fine grinding, small particles`],[`Kick`,`Size reduction ratio (logarithm)`,`Coarse crushing, large particles`],[`Bond`,`Crack length produced`,`Intermediate, rod and ball milling`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Mills`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Mill`,`Main action`,`Product size`,`Suits`],rows:[[`Cutter mill`,`Cutting`,`Coarse, 20 to 80 mesh`,`Tough fibrous crude drugs`],[`Hammer mill`,`Impact`,`Wide, coarse to fine`,`Brittle crystalline solids`],[`Ball mill`,`Impact plus attrition`,`Fine, down to a few micrometres`,`Abrasive, toxic, sterile work`],[`Fluid energy mill`,`Impact plus attrition by air`,`Micronised, about 1 to 20 micrometres`,`Heat-sensitive, high-value drugs`],[`Colloid mill`,`Shear in a narrow gap`,`Fine dispersion, wet only`,`Suspensions and emulsions`],[`End runner mill`,`Crushing and shearing by a pestle`,`Fine`,`Soft, sticky solids and ointments`],[`Edge runner mill`,`Crushing and shearing by heavy stones`,`Very fine`,`Tough and brittle solids`],[`Roller mill`,`Compression between rollers`,`Fine, smooth`,`Ointments, pastes, semisolids`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Hammer mill: swinging or fixed hammers on a rotor inside a screened chamber. The product size is set by the screen aperture and the rotor speed. Not used for abrasive or sticky material.`,`Ball mill: a rotating cylinder part-filled with balls. Because it is a closed vessel it is the mill of choice for toxic, hygroscopic or sterile products, and it can grind wet or dry.`,`Fluid energy mill (micronizer): particles are accelerated by compressed air or steam into a circular chamber and break by hitting each other. No moving parts, no heat build-up, and it micronises directly.`,`Colloid mill: rotor and stator separated by a very small adjustable gap; it works only on liquids and is the standard machine for emulsions and suspensions.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Critical speed of a ball mill`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Nc = (1 / 2 pi) x sqrt(g / (R - r))`,caption:`Critical speed in revolutions per second. R = radius of the mill, r = radius of the ball, g = 9.81 m/s2. At this speed centrifugal force holds the balls against the wall and no grinding occurs.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Nc (rpm) = 42.3 / sqrt(D - d)`,caption:`The same equation with the mill diameter D and ball diameter d in metres, giving revolutions per minute directly.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Operate at 50 to 80 percent of critical speed`,text:`Too slow and the balls merely roll (cascading, mainly attrition). At or above critical speed they centrifuge and nothing is ground. Optimum grinding, the cataracting regime, is at roughly two thirds of the critical speed.`}]},{topicId:`pe-size-reduction-separation`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions are numerical substitutions into the energy laws and the critical speed equation, plus the details of standard sieves and of separation by settling velocity.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Worked numericals`},{kind:`para`,text:`Rittinger: with Kr = 100 kJ mm per kg, reducing a powder from 2 mm to 0.5 mm needs E = 100 x (1/0.5 - 1/2) = 100 x (2 - 0.5) = 150 kJ/kg. Reducing the same material from 0.5 mm to 0.25 mm needs 100 x (4 - 2) = 200 kJ/kg, more energy for a smaller absolute change, which is Rittinger's point.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Kick: because the law depends only on the ratio, the energy to go from 10 mm to 5 mm is exactly the same as from 5 mm to 2.5 mm, since ln 2 appears in both. If that first step needs 20 kJ/kg, so does the second.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Bond: with a work index Wi = 12 kWh per ton, feed 80 percent passing 10000 micrometres and product 80 percent passing 100 micrometres, W = 10 x 12 x (1/sqrt(100) - 1/sqrt(10000)) = 120 x (0.1 - 0.01) = 10.8 kWh per ton.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Critical speed: a ball mill of internal diameter 1.2 m charged with 0.2 m balls has Nc = 42.3 / sqrt(1.2 - 0.2) = 42.3 / 1 = 42.3 rpm. It would be run at about 25 to 30 rpm, that is 60 to 70 percent of critical.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Sieves and sieving`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A standard sieve is designated by a number that relates to the number of meshes per linear inch, so a higher sieve number means a finer aperture. Sieve series are built so that successive apertures follow a fixed ratio.`,`Sieving methods include agitation by hand or by mechanical shaker, brushing, centrifugal action, and air-jet sieving for very fine powders.`,`Errors arise from overloading the sieve, sieving for too short a time, abrasion or blinding of the mesh, and from cohesive or electrostatic powders.`,`The Indian Pharmacopoeia grades powders as coarse, moderately coarse, moderately fine, fine and very fine, each grade defined by the sieve that all the powder must pass and, for the coarser grades, by a limit on how much may pass a second finer sieve.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Size separation without sieves`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Method`,`Fluid used`,`Basis of separation`],rows:[[`Sieving`,`None`,`Aperture of the mesh`],[`Cyclone separator`,`Air (gas)`,`Centrifugal force versus drag`],[`Elutriation`,`Rising air or water`,`Terminal settling velocity`],[`Sedimentation`,`Still liquid`,`Settling under gravity`],[`Air (double cone) classifier`,`Air stream`,`Combined gravity and centrifugal action`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Cyclone separator: the dust-laden air enters tangentially at the top of a conical vessel and spirals downwards. Heavy and large particles are thrown to the wall, lose momentum and fall into the receiver, while fines leave with the air through a central outlet at the top. There are no moving parts.`,`Elutriation: a fluid is passed upwards through the powder. Particles whose terminal settling velocity is less than the fluid velocity are carried over; the rest settle. Raising the fluid velocity in steps gives successive size fractions.`,`Sedimentation methods, including the Andreasen pipette, separate and size particles by allowing them to settle in a liquid and applying Stokes law.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`v = d^2 (rho_s - rho_l) g / (18 mu)`,caption:`Stokes law for terminal settling velocity of a sphere. d = particle diameter, rho_s and rho_l = densities of solid and liquid, mu = liquid viscosity. Velocity varies with the square of diameter.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Fluid energy mill for thermolabile drugs`,text:`Any question about micronising a heat-sensitive or high-potency drug to a few micrometres in one step points to the fluid energy mill, because the expanding compressed air cools rather than heats the charge.`}]},{topicId:`pe-filtration-mixing`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Filtration is the separation of solids from a fluid by passing the mixture through a porous barrier that holds back the solid. Mixing is the opposite kind of operation: it brings two or more separate materials together until every sample taken has the same composition. Both are everyday operations in a formulation plant.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Words to define first`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Slurry: the feed, that is the suspension of solid in liquid presented to the filter.`,`Filtrate: the clear liquid that has passed through the filter.`,`Filter cake: the layer of solid that builds up on the filter medium.`,`Filter medium: the porous barrier itself, for example a cloth, a membrane or a bed of sand.`,`Filter aid: an inert porous powder added to the slurry, or laid down first as a pre-coat, to keep the cake open and prevent blinding of the medium.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`How a filter actually retains particles`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Straining or sieving: particles larger than the pore are simply held on the surface.`,`Entanglement (impingement): particles smaller than the pore become trapped in the thickness of a fibrous medium.`,`Attractive forces: very fine particles are adsorbed onto the medium by electrostatic and van der Waals forces, which is how a depth filter can hold particles far smaller than its pores.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`Surface filters, such as a membrane, retain everything above the rated pore size on the face and are used when a sharp cut-off is needed. Depth filters, such as a sintered or fibrous bed, hold particles throughout their thickness and take a much higher dirt load before clogging.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Mixing in one line`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Positive mixture: components mix spontaneously and irreversibly, for example two miscible gases or two miscible liquids. No energy input is really needed.`,`Negative mixture: components separate again on standing, for example a suspension or an emulsion, so energy must be supplied continuously or the product stabilised.`,`Neutral mixture: the components neither mix on their own nor separate on their own, for example a mixed dry powder or an ointment.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Clarification is not cake filtration`,text:`Clarification removes a very small amount of solid from a large volume of liquid and the liquid is the product. Cake filtration handles a thick slurry and the solid may be the product. The equipment chosen is completely different, so read what the question calls the product.`}]},{topicId:`pe-filtration-mixing`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The exam level needs the rate equation for filtration, the factors you can change to speed a filter up, the standard filters with their advantages, and the equipment used for solids, liquids and semisolids.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Filtration theory`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`dV/dt = K A delta P / (mu L)`,caption:`Darcy equation. dV/dt = rate of filtration (m3/s), K = permeability of the cake, A = filtration area (m2), delta P = pressure drop across the cake (Pa), mu = viscosity of the filtrate (Pa s), L = thickness of the cake (m).`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Rate rises in direct proportion to the area and to the pressure drop.`,`Rate falls as the filtrate becomes more viscous, so warming the slurry is a cheap way to filter faster.`,`Rate falls as the cake thickens, which is why filtration rate always drops with time in a batch filter and why continuous filters scrape the cake off.`,`A compressible cake collapses under high pressure and the permeability K falls, so simply raising the pressure may not help. A filter aid keeps such a cake porous.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Filter media and filter aids`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Filter media in use: woven cloth of cotton, wool, nylon, polypropylene or polyester; non-woven felts; woven wire mesh; sintered metal and sintered glass; porous ceramic; and polymeric membranes.`,`An ideal medium is chemically inert, mechanically strong, retains the solid without blinding, gives a clear filtrate, releases the cake cleanly and does not shed fibres or adsorb the drug.`,`Filter aids commonly used are kieselguhr (diatomaceous earth), perlite, purified cellulose, talc, charcoal and bentonite.`,`Filter aids are applied as a pre-coat, laid down first as a thin layer, and as a body mix stirred into the slurry so the cake stays porous throughout.`,`A filter aid must never be used when the solid itself is the wanted product, because the two cannot then be separated, and never when it would adsorb the active ingredient.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Filters and their advantages`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Filter`,`Mode`,`Chief advantage`],rows:[[`Plate and frame press`,`Batch, pressure`,`Very large area in a small floor space`],[`Rotary drum vacuum filter`,`Continuous, vacuum`,`Automatic, handles large volumes of slurry`],[`Meta filter (edge filter)`,`Batch, pressure`,`No cloth needed, robust, easily cleaned`],[`Cartridge filter`,`In-line, pressure`,`Disposable, no cross-contamination`],[`Membrane filter`,`Surface, pressure`,`Sterile filtrate, absolute pore rating`],[`Sintered glass filter`,`Small scale, vacuum`,`Inert, sheds no fibres, reusable`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Plate and frame press: alternating plates and hollow frames covered with cloth. Cheap, versatile and gives a large area, but it is a batch operation, labour intensive to dismantle and clean, and cloth wear can let solids through.`,`Rotary drum vacuum filter: a drum covered with cloth dips into a slurry trough; vacuum inside sucks filtrate through while the cake forms, and the cake is washed, dried and scraped off by a knife on each revolution. It is the classic continuous filter for heavy slurries but is expensive and unsuitable for very fine or slimy solids.`,`Meta filter: a series of precision metal rings threaded on a fluted rod, leaving fine edge channels. It needs no filter cloth, tolerates high pressure, is easily cleaned by back-flushing and is used with a filter aid for clarifying syrups and injections.`,`Membrane filter: thin polymeric film of defined pore size. A 0.22 micrometre membrane is used for sterilising filtration of thermolabile solutions and for sterility testing; membranes are non-shedding but have low dirt capacity, so a pre-filter is usually fitted.`,`Sintered glass filter: fused glass granules in a funnel, graded by porosity number. Used for small volumes and corrosive liquids because it is chemically inert and does not shed fibres.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Mixing equipment`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Material to be mixed`,`Equipment`,`Note`],rows:[[`Dry solids`,`Double cone, V (twin shell), cube blender`,`Tumbling action, free-flowing powders`],[`Dry solids, cohesive`,`Ribbon blender, planetary mixer`,`Agitator forces movement, no tumbling`],[`Low viscosity liquids`,`Propeller mixer`,`Axial flow, high speed, large volumes`],[`Medium viscosity liquids`,`Turbine mixer`,`Radial flow, good for emulsions`],[`Viscous liquids, syrups`,`Paddle mixer`,`Low speed, poor top-to-bottom flow`],[`Semisolids, ointments`,`Sigma blade (Z blade) mixer, planetary mixer`,`High shear kneading action`],[`Ointments, pastes`,`Triple roller mill`,`Shear between rollers, removes grittiness`],[`Emulsions, suspensions`,`Colloid mill, silverson homogeniser`,`Very high shear in a narrow gap`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`0.22 micrometre means sterilising grade`,text:`A question that mentions filtration of a heat-labile injection, or the sterility test, is asking about the membrane filter of 0.22 micrometre nominal pore size. This is one of the most repeated single facts in the whole engineering paper.`}]},{topicId:`pe-filtration-mixing`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced material covers cake and medium resistance handled together, the mechanisms and failures of solids mixing, and the dimensionless numbers used in scaling up an agitated vessel.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`dV/dt = A delta P / (mu (Rc + Rm))`,caption:`The Darcy equation written with resistances. Rc = resistance of the cake, which grows as filtration proceeds, Rm = resistance of the filter medium, which is constant.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Early in a run the medium resistance dominates, so the rate is nearly constant. Once the cake is established Rc dominates and the rate falls off steadily.`,`Constant pressure filtration is the usual laboratory and small-plant mode; constant rate filtration is used when a pump of fixed displacement feeds the filter and the pressure is allowed to climb.`,`Doubling the pressure drop doubles the instantaneous rate only for an incompressible cake. For a compressible cake the gain is much less than proportional, and beyond a point the rate can even fall.`,`Halving the filtrate viscosity, for example by warming the syrup, doubles the rate at the same pressure. This is often the cheapest intervention available.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Mechanisms of solids mixing`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Convective mixing: whole groups of particles are moved from one place to another, as by a ribbon blender. It dominates early and gives coarse mixing.`,`Shear mixing: slip planes form within the powder bed and layers slide over one another.`,`Diffusive mixing: individual particles move randomly across a freshly created surface, as in a tumbling blender. It is slow but gives the final, truly random mix.`]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Segregation, or demixing, undoes a good mix. It is caused chiefly by differences in particle size, and also by differences in density and in shape.`,`Trajectory segregation, percolation of fines through the bed, and elutriation of fines by air are the three named mechanisms.`,`Remedies are to reduce the size difference by milling or granulating, to avoid vibration and long chute drops, and to keep the mixing time to the optimum rather than the longest.`,`Over-mixing is a real fault: mixing beyond the optimum time can allow segregation to increase again, and for a lubricant such as magnesium stearate it over-coats the granules and slows disintegration and dissolution.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Dimensionless groups for agitated vessels`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Group`,`Expression`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`Reynolds number`,`D^2 N rho / mu`,`Inertial versus viscous force`],[`Froude number`,`D N^2 / g`,`Inertial versus gravitational force`],[`Power number`,`P / (rho N^3 D^5)`,`Power drawn by the impeller`]],caption:`D = impeller diameter, N = revolutions per second, P = power. The Froude number matters only in an unbaffled tank where a vortex can form.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Baffles fitted to the wall of a mixing tank convert swirling into vertical top-to-bottom flow and prevent vortex formation, which would otherwise entrain air and cut mixing efficiency.`,`For an unbaffled tank the impeller may instead be mounted off-centre or at an angle to break the vortex.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Match the mixer to the consistency`,text:`Free-flowing powder means a tumbling blender such as the double cone or V blender. Cohesive powder or a wet mass means a ribbon or planetary mixer. Ointment means a sigma blade mixer or a triple roller mill. Emulsion means a colloid mill or homogeniser.`}]}],t=[{id:`q-pe-fluid-flow-1`,topicId:`pe-fluid-flow`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The unit of Reynolds number is:`,options:[`metre per second`,`pascal second`,`It is dimensionless`,`kilogram per cubic metre`],answer:2,explanation:`Reynolds number is the ratio of inertial force to viscous force, so every unit cancels and it is dimensionless. Pascal second is the unit of viscosity, which is only one of the four terms inside Re.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pe-fluid-flow-2`,topicId:`pe-fluid-flow`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Flow of a fluid through a circular pipe is described as laminar when the Reynolds number is:`,options:[`Less than 2100`,`Between 2100 and 4000`,`More than 4000`,`More than 10000`],answer:0,explanation:`For flow in a circular pipe, Re below 2100 is laminar, 2100 to 4000 is the transitional band and above 4000 is turbulent. The 2100 figure is the standard critical Reynolds number; some texts round it to 2000, so treat anything in that region as the laminar-to-transitional boundary rather than memorising a hard line. The 2100 to 4000 option is the transitional range, not the laminar one.`},{id:`q-pe-fluid-flow-3`,topicId:`pe-fluid-flow`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Water of density 1000 kg/m3 and viscosity 0.001 Pa s flows at 1.5 m/s through a pipe of internal diameter 25 mm. The Reynolds number and the type of flow are:`,options:[`3750, laminar`,`37500, turbulent`,`375000, turbulent`,`1500, laminar`],answer:1,explanation:`Re = D v rho / mu = (0.025 x 1.5 x 1000) / 0.001 = 37.5 / 0.001 = 37500, which is above 4000 so the flow is turbulent. The value 3750 comes from forgetting to convert 25 mm into 0.025 m correctly and would wrongly suggest a different regime.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pe-fluid-flow-4`,topicId:`pe-fluid-flow`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which device measures the velocity of a fluid at a single point in the cross-section rather than the total rate of flow?`,options:[`Orifice meter`,`Venturi meter`,`Rotameter`,`Pitot tube`],answer:3,explanation:`The pitot tube compares impact pressure with static pressure at the point where its nose sits, so it gives a point velocity and the pipe must be traversed to get the average. Orifice and venturi meters sense a differential across the whole cross-section and therefore give the average velocity directly.`},{id:`q-pe-fluid-flow-5`,topicId:`pe-fluid-flow`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The flow meter that works at a constant pressure drop with a variable area of flow is the:`,options:[`Rotameter`,`Orifice meter`,`Venturi meter`,`Pitot tube`],answer:0,explanation:`In a rotameter the float rises in a tapered tube until the annular area gives a drag that balances its weight, so the area varies while the pressure drop across the float stays constant. Orifice and venturi meters are variable-head meters: their area is fixed and the pressure drop changes with flow.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pe-fluid-flow-6`,topicId:`pe-fluid-flow`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A pipe carrying a liquid at 2 m/s narrows so that its diameter is halved. The velocity in the narrow section becomes:`,options:[`1 m/s`,`4 m/s`,`8 m/s`,`16 m/s`],answer:2,explanation:`By continuity A1 v1 = A2 v2, and area varies as the square of diameter, so halving the diameter quarters the area and the velocity must rise four-fold: 2 x 4 = 8 m/s. Choosing 4 m/s is the common error of doubling the velocity instead of quadrupling it.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pe-fluid-flow-7`,topicId:`pe-fluid-flow`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A liquid flows through a pipe at a Reynolds number of 1000. The Fanning friction factor is:`,options:[`0.064`,`0.016`,`0.0016`,`It cannot be found without the roughness`],answer:1,explanation:`Re = 1000 is laminar, and in laminar flow the Fanning friction factor is f = 16/Re = 16/1000 = 0.016. The value 0.064 is 64/Re, which is the Darcy friction factor and is exactly four times the Fanning value; roughness matters only in turbulent flow.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pe-fluid-flow-8`,topicId:`pe-fluid-flow`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A mercury differential manometer (mercury density 13600 kg/m3) connected across an orifice in a water line reads 10 cm. The pressure difference is approximately:`,options:[`13.3 kPa`,`12.4 kPa`,`9.81 kPa`,`1.24 kPa`],answer:1,explanation:`delta P = (rho_m - rho_f) g R = (13600 - 1000) x 9.81 x 0.10 = 12600 x 0.981 = 12361 Pa, about 12.4 kPa. The answer 13.3 kPa comes from using the mercury density alone and forgetting to subtract the density of the water standing above it.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pe-heat-evaporation-1`,topicId:`pe-heat-evaporation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Fourier's law describes heat transfer by:`,options:[`Convection`,`Conduction`,`Radiation`,`Evaporation`],answer:1,explanation:`Fourier's law, Q = kA(T1 - T2)/x, contains thermal conductivity and thickness, which are properties of a solid conducting path. Newton's law of cooling covers convection and the Stefan-Boltzmann law covers radiation.`},{id:`q-pe-heat-evaporation-2`,topicId:`pe-heat-evaporation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The economy of an evaporator is defined as:`,options:[`Kilograms of water evaporated per hour`,`Kilograms of vapour produced per kilogram of steam used`,`Kilogram of steam used per hour`,`Heat transferred per unit area per hour`],answer:1,explanation:`Economy is a dimensionless efficiency of steam usage, that is vapour produced divided by steam consumed. Kilograms of water evaporated per hour is the capacity, which measures throughput and is a completely separate quantity.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pe-heat-evaporation-3`,topicId:`pe-heat-evaporation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A flat wall of area 4 m2 and thickness 20 mm has a thermal conductivity of 0.5 W/m K. If the temperature difference across it is 50 K, the rate of heat flow is:`,options:[`100 W`,`500 W`,`5000 W`,`50000 W`],answer:2,explanation:`Q = kA(delta T)/x = 0.5 x 4 x 50 / 0.02 = 100 / 0.02 = 5000 W. The answer 100 W is what you get if the thickness is left in millimetres instead of metres, which is the usual slip in this calculation.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pe-heat-evaporation-4`,topicId:`pe-heat-evaporation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Inside and outside film coefficients are 500 and 2000 W/m2 K and the wall resistance is negligible. The overall heat transfer coefficient is:`,options:[`2500 W/m2 K`,`1250 W/m2 K`,`400 W/m2 K`,`250 W/m2 K`],answer:2,explanation:`1/U = 1/500 + 1/2000 = 0.002 + 0.0005 = 0.0025, so U = 400 W/m2 K. Adding the coefficients to give 2500 is wrong because resistances, not conductances, add in series, and the answer must always be smaller than the smaller of the two coefficients.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pe-heat-evaporation-5`,topicId:`pe-heat-evaporation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Compared with a single effect of the same total heating area, a triple effect evaporator:`,options:[`Has about three times the capacity and the same economy`,`Has about the same capacity and about three times the economy`,`Has three times both capacity and economy`,`Has one third the capacity and one third the economy`],answer:1,explanation:`The vapour from one effect heats the next, so one kilogram of steam evaporates roughly one kilogram of water in each effect and the economy rises with the number of effects. The available temperature drop is shared out between the effects, so capacity stays roughly the same; the claim that capacity triples is the classic distractor.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pe-heat-evaporation-6`,topicId:`pe-heat-evaporation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A viscous liquid that tends to deposit scale on the heating surface is best concentrated in a:`,options:[`Short tube (calandria) evaporator`,`Steam jacketed open pan`,`Forced circulation evaporator`,`Horizontal tube evaporator`],answer:2,explanation:`A pump drives the liquid through the tubes at high velocity in a forced circulation evaporator, which raises the film coefficient and sweeps solids away before they can deposit. Natural circulation units such as the calandria rely on density differences and stall with viscous or scaling liquids.`},{id:`q-pe-heat-evaporation-7`,topicId:`pe-heat-evaporation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In a counter-current exchanger the terminal temperature differences are 60 K and 20 K. The log mean temperature difference is closest to:`,options:[`40.0 K`,`36.4 K`,`34.6 K`,`30.0 K`],answer:1,explanation:`(delta T)lm = (60 - 20) / ln(60/20) = 40 / ln 3 = 40 / 1.0986 = 36.4 K. The value 40.0 K is the arithmetic mean, which always overestimates the true driving force and is only a fair approximation when the two terminal differences are close together.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pe-heat-evaporation-8`,topicId:`pe-heat-evaporation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A multiple effect unit evaporates 7500 kg of water per hour at a steam economy of 2.5. The steam consumption is:`,options:[`18750 kg/h`,`7500 kg/h`,`3000 kg/h`,`1875 kg/h`],answer:2,explanation:`Economy = vapour produced / steam supplied, so steam = 7500 / 2.5 = 3000 kg/h. Multiplying instead of dividing gives 18750 kg/h, which would mean the unit used more steam than the water it removed, the opposite of what multiple effect working achieves.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pe-drying-humidity-1`,topicId:`pe-drying-humidity`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`During the constant rate period of drying, the temperature of the wet surface is equal to the:`,options:[`Dry bulb temperature of the air`,`Dew point of the air`,`Wet bulb temperature of the air`,`Boiling point of the liquid`],answer:2,explanation:`All the heat arriving at the fully wetted surface is used as latent heat of vaporisation, so the surface settles at the wet bulb temperature of the air. The dry bulb temperature is reached only later, once the surface has dried out in the falling rate period.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pe-drying-humidity-2`,topicId:`pe-drying-humidity`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The moisture content of a solid at the end of the constant rate period is called the:`,options:[`Critical moisture content`,`Equilibrium moisture content`,`Bound moisture content`,`Free moisture content`],answer:0,explanation:`The critical moisture content marks the point where the continuous surface film first breaks and the falling rate period begins. The equilibrium moisture content is the very different end point of drying, where the rate has fallen to zero.`},{id:`q-pe-drying-humidity-3`,topicId:`pe-drying-humidity`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Air contains water vapour at a partial pressure of 12 mm Hg at a temperature where the saturation vapour pressure of water is 30 mm Hg. The relative humidity is:`,options:[`30 percent`,`40 percent`,`60 percent`,`250 percent`],answer:1,explanation:`RH = (p/ps) x 100 = (12/30) x 100 = 40 percent. Dividing the wrong way round gives 250 percent, which is impossible because relative humidity can never exceed 100 percent.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pe-drying-humidity-4`,topicId:`pe-drying-humidity`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which fraction of the moisture in a wet solid can never be removed by drying with air of a given temperature and humidity?`,options:[`Free moisture`,`Unbound moisture`,`Critical moisture`,`Equilibrium moisture`],answer:3,explanation:`The equilibrium moisture content is the moisture the solid holds when it is already in equilibrium with that air, so there is no driving force left to remove it. Free moisture is by definition the part above the equilibrium value and is precisely what drying does remove.`},{id:`q-pe-drying-humidity-5`,topicId:`pe-drying-humidity`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A thermolabile drug solution must be converted into a free-flowing dry powder in a single continuous step. The dryer of choice is the:`,options:[`Spray dryer`,`Tray dryer`,`Drum dryer`,`Fluidised bed dryer`],answer:0,explanation:`Spray drying atomises the solution into a hot air stream; each droplet dries in seconds while staying near the wet bulb temperature, so the product is barely heated and emerges as a spherical free-flowing powder. A fluidised bed dryer needs an already granular wet solid as feed, not a solution.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pe-drying-humidity-6`,topicId:`pe-drying-humidity`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The principle underlying freeze drying is:`,options:[`Evaporation of water at reduced boiling point`,`Absorption of water by a desiccant`,`Sublimation of ice below the triple point of water`,`Condensation of water on a cold surface only`],answer:2,explanation:`The product is frozen and the pressure lowered below the triple point, so ice passes directly to vapour without ever becoming liquid; the condenser then traps that vapour. Evaporation at a reduced boiling point describes vacuum drying, in which the water does pass through the liquid state.`},{id:`q-pe-drying-humidity-7`,topicId:`pe-drying-humidity`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The partial pressure of water vapour in air at a total pressure of 760 mm Hg is 20 mm Hg. The absolute humidity in kg water per kg dry air is closest to:`,options:[`0.0164`,`0.0168`,`0.0264`,`0.622`],answer:1,explanation:`H = 0.622 x p/(P - p) = 0.622 x 20/(760 - 20) = 0.622 x 0.02703 = 0.0168 kg per kg dry air. Using 760 instead of 740 in the denominator gives about 0.0164, a small but avoidable error; the point of the formula is that the denominator is the partial pressure of the dry air alone.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pe-drying-humidity-8`,topicId:`pe-drying-humidity`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which statement about the falling rate period of drying is correct?`,options:[`The rate is controlled mainly by the velocity of the drying air`,`Only unbound moisture is removed during this period`,`The surface stays at the wet bulb temperature throughout`,`The rate is controlled mainly by movement of moisture inside the solid`],answer:3,explanation:`Once the surface film has broken, the limiting step is diffusion or capillary movement of water from the interior to the surface, so the thickness of the solid matters and the air velocity has little effect. Air velocity is decisive only in the constant rate period, when the external conditions control the rate.`},{id:`q-pe-size-reduction-separation-1`,topicId:`pe-size-reduction-separation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which mill is most suitable for reducing the size of tough, fibrous crude drugs?`,options:[`Ball mill`,`Fluid energy mill`,`Colloid mill`,`Cutter mill`],answer:3,explanation:`A cutter mill uses sharp rotating knives against fixed blades, and cutting is the only mechanism that works on tough fibrous material. Impact mills such as the ball mill need a brittle material that fractures, and a fibrous drug simply absorbs the blow.`},{id:`q-pe-size-reduction-separation-2`,topicId:`pe-size-reduction-separation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`According to Rittinger's law, the energy required for size reduction is proportional to the:`,options:[`New surface area produced`,`Logarithm of the size reduction ratio`,`Square root of the final particle size`,`Mass of the material only`],answer:0,explanation:`Rittinger's law states that energy is proportional to the new surface created, which is why it fits fine grinding where surface area increases enormously. The logarithm of the size reduction ratio is Kick's law, which fits coarse crushing instead.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pe-size-reduction-separation-3`,topicId:`pe-size-reduction-separation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A ball mill of internal diameter 1.6 m is charged with balls of diameter 0.1 m. Its critical speed is closest to:`,options:[`26.4 rpm`,`30.0 rpm`,`34.5 rpm`,`42.3 rpm`],answer:2,explanation:`Nc = 42.3 / sqrt(D - d) = 42.3 / sqrt(1.6 - 0.1) = 42.3 / 1.2247 = 34.5 rpm. Using 42.3 alone ignores the term (D - d) and would only be right if the difference were exactly 1 m.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pe-size-reduction-separation-4`,topicId:`pe-size-reduction-separation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A heat-sensitive, high-potency drug must be micronised to a few micrometres. The most appropriate mill is the:`,options:[`Hammer mill`,`Fluid energy mill`,`Edge runner mill`,`Roller mill`],answer:1,explanation:`In a fluid energy mill the particles are accelerated by compressed air and break by colliding with each other; the expanding gas cools the charge, so no heat is generated, and it reaches the micrometre range in one pass. A hammer mill generates considerable frictional heat and cannot reliably reach that size.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pe-size-reduction-separation-5`,topicId:`pe-size-reduction-separation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Using Kick's law, if 20 kJ/kg is needed to reduce a material from 10 mm to 5 mm, the energy needed to reduce it further from 5 mm to 2.5 mm is:`,options:[`10 kJ/kg`,`14.1 kJ/kg`,`20 kJ/kg`,`40 kJ/kg`],answer:2,explanation:`Kick's law gives E = Kk ln(d1/d2) and both steps have the same size reduction ratio of 2, so both require Kk ln 2, that is 20 kJ/kg. The answer 40 kJ/kg would be right under Rittinger's law, where energy depends on the reciprocals of the sizes and therefore rises as the particles get smaller.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pe-size-reduction-separation-6`,topicId:`pe-size-reduction-separation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A cyclone separator separates particles from a gas stream principally by:`,options:[`Centrifugal force generated by a spiral path`,`A rising stream of fluid that carries fines upward`,`The aperture of a wire mesh`,`Electrostatic attraction to a charged plate`],answer:0,explanation:`Dust-laden air enters tangentially and spirals down the conical body, and the centrifugal force throws the larger, heavier particles to the wall while fines leave with the air at the top. A rising stream of fluid carrying fines upward describes elutriation, which is a different separation method.`},{id:`q-pe-size-reduction-separation-7`,topicId:`pe-size-reduction-separation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Taking Rittinger's constant as 60 kJ mm per kg, the energy required to reduce a powder from 4 mm to 1 mm is:`,options:[`15 kJ/kg`,`30 kJ/kg`,`40 kJ/kg`,`45 kJ/kg`],answer:3,explanation:`E = Kr (1/d2 - 1/d1) = 60 x (1/1 - 1/4) = 60 x 0.75 = 45 kJ/kg. Reversing the bracket to (1/d1 - 1/d2) gives a negative value, and using the size difference of 3 mm instead of the difference of the reciprocals gives none of the correct terms.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pe-size-reduction-separation-8`,topicId:`pe-size-reduction-separation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A material with a Bond work index of 12 kWh per ton is ground from a feed 80 percent passing 10000 micrometres to a product 80 percent passing 100 micrometres. The work required is:`,options:[`1.08 kWh/ton`,`10.8 kWh/ton`,`12.0 kWh/ton`,`108 kWh/ton`],answer:1,explanation:`W = 10 Wi (1/sqrt(P80) - 1/sqrt(F80)); here 1/sqrt(100) = 0.1 and 1/sqrt(10000) = 0.01, so W = 10 x 12 x (0.1 - 0.01) = 120 x 0.09 = 10.8 kWh/ton. Forgetting the factor of 10 that the Bond equation carries gives 1.08 kWh/ton.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pe-filtration-mixing-1`,topicId:`pe-filtration-mixing`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which of the following is used as a filter aid?`,options:[`Sodium lauryl sulphate`,`Kieselguhr`,`Magnesium stearate`,`Sodium metabisulphite`],answer:1,explanation:`Kieselguhr, or diatomaceous earth, is an inert porous powder applied as a pre-coat or body mix to keep the cake permeable. Magnesium stearate is a tablet lubricant and sodium metabisulphite an antioxidant; neither has any filtration function.`},{id:`q-pe-filtration-mixing-2`,topicId:`pe-filtration-mixing`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which filter operates continuously, forming, washing and discharging cake on every revolution?`,options:[`Plate and frame press`,`Meta filter`,`Rotary drum vacuum filter`,`Sintered glass filter`],answer:2,explanation:`The rotary drum vacuum filter picks up cake as the drum dips into the slurry trough and a knife scrapes it off before the surface re-enters, so the cycle never stops. The plate and frame press must be shut down and dismantled to remove its cake, making it strictly a batch filter.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pe-filtration-mixing-3`,topicId:`pe-filtration-mixing`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`According to the Darcy equation, the rate of filtration is inversely proportional to:`,options:[`Viscosity of the filtrate and thickness of the cake`,`Area of the filter and pressure drop`,`Pressure drop and permeability of the cake`,`Area of the filter and viscosity of the filtrate`],answer:0,explanation:`In dV/dt = K A delta P / (mu L), viscosity and cake thickness sit in the denominator, so the rate falls as either increases; area and pressure drop sit in the numerator and raise the rate. This is why a syrup filters faster when warmed and why a thick cake must be removed.`},{id:`q-pe-filtration-mixing-4`,topicId:`pe-filtration-mixing`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The chief advantage of the meta filter over the plate and frame press is that it:`,options:[`Handles far heavier solid loads`,`Works entirely without applied pressure`,`Gives a sterile filtrate without any further step`,`Needs no filter cloth and is easily cleaned`],answer:3,explanation:`The meta filter is built from precision metal rings whose edges form the filtering channels, so there is no cloth to wear, clog or replace, and it can be cleaned in place by back-flushing. It is a clarifying filter for light solid loads, not a machine for heavy cakes, and it does not sterilise.`},{id:`q-pe-filtration-mixing-5`,topicId:`pe-filtration-mixing`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The nominal pore size of a membrane filter used for sterilising filtration of a thermolabile injection is:`,options:[`0.45 micrometre`,`0.22 micrometre`,`1.2 micrometre`,`5 micrometre`],answer:1,explanation:`A membrane of 0.22 micrometre nominal pore size is the accepted sterilising grade because it retains bacteria such as Pseudomonas diminuta. A 0.45 micrometre membrane is a clarifying or bioburden-reduction grade and is not accepted as sterilising.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pe-filtration-mixing-6`,topicId:`pe-filtration-mixing`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The mixer of choice for preparing an ointment or a wet mass of high consistency is the:`,options:[`Double cone blender`,`Propeller mixer`,`Sigma blade mixer`,`V (twin shell) blender`],answer:2,explanation:`The sigma or Z blade mixer has two heavy interlocking arms that knead and shear a stiff mass, which is exactly what a semisolid needs. Double cone and V blenders rely on tumbling and work only on free-flowing dry powders, which a paste is not.`},{id:`q-pe-filtration-mixing-7`,topicId:`pe-filtration-mixing`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A filter delivers 120 litres per hour. The slurry is warmed so that the filtrate viscosity halves, and the applied pressure difference is raised 1.5 times. Assuming an incompressible cake of unchanged thickness, the new rate is:`,options:[`180 L/h`,`240 L/h`,`90 L/h`,`360 L/h`],answer:3,explanation:`Rate is directly proportional to delta P and inversely proportional to viscosity, so the rate is multiplied by 1.5 and by 2, giving 120 x 3 = 360 L/h. Choosing 180 L/h counts only the pressure change and ignores that halving viscosity doubles the rate on its own.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pe-filtration-mixing-8`,topicId:`pe-filtration-mixing`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Prolonged mixing of a granule blend with magnesium stearate beyond the optimum time is undesirable mainly because it:`,options:[`Over-coats the granules with hydrophobic lubricant and slows disintegration and dissolution`,`Increases the moisture content of the granules`,`Converts convective mixing into diffusive mixing`,`Raises the critical moisture content of the blend`],answer:0,explanation:`Magnesium stearate is hydrophobic and shears into a film over the granule surface, so extended blending waterproofs the tablet and retards disintegration and drug release; it also weakens the compact. Diffusive mixing is a normal late-stage mechanism and is not itself a defect.`}],n=[{id:`f-pe-fluid-flow-1`,topicId:`pe-fluid-flow`,front:`Reynolds number equation?`,back:`Re = D v rho / mu (diameter x velocity x density / viscosity); dimensionless.`},{id:`f-pe-fluid-flow-2`,topicId:`pe-fluid-flow`,front:`Reynolds ranges for pipe flow?`,back:`Below 2000 laminar, 2000 to 4000 transitional, above 4000 turbulent.`},{id:`f-pe-fluid-flow-3`,topicId:`pe-fluid-flow`,front:`Which flow meter gives point velocity?`,back:`Pitot tube. Orifice and venturi give the average velocity.`},{id:`f-pe-fluid-flow-4`,topicId:`pe-fluid-flow`,front:`Which meter has variable area and constant pressure drop?`,back:`Rotameter.`},{id:`f-pe-fluid-flow-5`,topicId:`pe-fluid-flow`,front:`Bernoulli equation in head form?`,back:`P/(rho g) + v^2/(2g) + Z = constant.`},{id:`f-pe-fluid-flow-6`,topicId:`pe-fluid-flow`,front:`Fanning friction factor in laminar flow?`,back:`f = 16/Re (Darcy factor = 64/Re, four times larger).`},{id:`f-pe-heat-evaporation-1`,topicId:`pe-heat-evaporation`,front:`Fourier law of conduction?`,back:`Q = k A (T1 - T2) / x.`},{id:`f-pe-heat-evaporation-2`,topicId:`pe-heat-evaporation`,front:`Overall heat transfer coefficient equation?`,back:`1/U = 1/hi + x/k + 1/ho (resistances in series add).`},{id:`f-pe-heat-evaporation-3`,topicId:`pe-heat-evaporation`,front:`Economy versus capacity of an evaporator?`,back:`Economy = kg vapour per kg steam. Capacity = kg water evaporated per hour.`},{id:`f-pe-heat-evaporation-4`,topicId:`pe-heat-evaporation`,front:`What does multiple effect evaporation improve?`,back:`Steam economy rises roughly with the number of effects; capacity stays about the same.`},{id:`f-pe-heat-evaporation-5`,topicId:`pe-heat-evaporation`,front:`Evaporator for a viscous, scaling liquid?`,back:`Forced circulation evaporator.`},{id:`f-pe-heat-evaporation-6`,topicId:`pe-heat-evaporation`,front:`Log mean temperature difference formula?`,back:`(delta T)lm = (delta T1 - delta T2) / ln(delta T1 / delta T2).`},{id:`f-pe-drying-humidity-1`,topicId:`pe-drying-humidity`,front:`Relative humidity formula?`,back:`RH = (p / ps) x 100, using partial pressure over saturation vapour pressure.`},{id:`f-pe-drying-humidity-2`,topicId:`pe-drying-humidity`,front:`Absolute humidity of air formula?`,back:`H = 0.622 p / (P - p), in kg water per kg dry air.`},{id:`f-pe-drying-humidity-3`,topicId:`pe-drying-humidity`,front:`Critical moisture content is at which point?`,back:`The end of the constant rate period, where the falling rate period begins.`},{id:`f-pe-drying-humidity-4`,topicId:`pe-drying-humidity`,front:`Surface temperature during the constant rate period?`,back:`The wet bulb temperature of the drying air.`},{id:`f-pe-drying-humidity-5`,topicId:`pe-drying-humidity`,front:`Which moisture can never be removed by drying?`,back:`Equilibrium moisture content, for that air temperature and humidity.`},{id:`f-pe-drying-humidity-6`,topicId:`pe-drying-humidity`,front:`Dryer for vaccines and sera?`,back:`Freeze dryer: ice sublimes under vacuum below the triple point.`},{id:`f-pe-size-reduction-separation-1`,topicId:`pe-size-reduction-separation`,front:`Rittinger law equation?`,back:`E = Kr (1/d2 - 1/d1); energy proportional to new surface, best for fine grinding.`},{id:`f-pe-size-reduction-separation-2`,topicId:`pe-size-reduction-separation`,front:`Kick law equation?`,back:`E = Kk ln(d1/d2); depends only on the size reduction ratio, best for coarse crushing.`},{id:`f-pe-size-reduction-separation-3`,topicId:`pe-size-reduction-separation`,front:`Critical speed of a ball mill?`,back:`Nc = (1/2 pi) sqrt(g/(R - r)); in rpm, Nc = 42.3 / sqrt(D - d) with D and d in metres.`},{id:`f-pe-size-reduction-separation-4`,topicId:`pe-size-reduction-separation`,front:`Mill for micronising a heat-sensitive drug?`,back:`Fluid energy mill (micronizer), about 1 to 20 micrometres, no heat generated.`},{id:`f-pe-size-reduction-separation-5`,topicId:`pe-size-reduction-separation`,front:`Mill for tough fibrous crude drugs?`,back:`Cutter mill, which works by cutting rather than impact.`},{id:`f-pe-size-reduction-separation-6`,topicId:`pe-size-reduction-separation`,front:`Basis of separation in elutriation?`,back:`Terminal settling velocity: a rising fluid carries away particles that settle more slowly than it rises.`},{id:`f-pe-filtration-mixing-1`,topicId:`pe-filtration-mixing`,front:`Darcy equation for filtration rate?`,back:`dV/dt = K A delta P / (mu L).`},{id:`f-pe-filtration-mixing-2`,topicId:`pe-filtration-mixing`,front:`Name three filter aids.`,back:`Kieselguhr (diatomaceous earth), purified cellulose and talc; also perlite, charcoal and bentonite.`},{id:`f-pe-filtration-mixing-3`,topicId:`pe-filtration-mixing`,front:`Sterilising grade membrane pore size?`,back:`0.22 micrometre.`},{id:`f-pe-filtration-mixing-4`,topicId:`pe-filtration-mixing`,front:`Which filter needs no filter cloth?`,back:`Meta filter (edge filter), built from precision metal rings on a fluted rod.`},{id:`f-pe-filtration-mixing-5`,topicId:`pe-filtration-mixing`,front:`Three mechanisms of solids mixing?`,back:`Convective, shear and diffusive mixing.`},{id:`f-pe-filtration-mixing-6`,topicId:`pe-filtration-mixing`,front:`Equipment for mixing an ointment?`,back:`Sigma (Z) blade mixer or planetary mixer, finished on a triple roller mill.`}];export{n as flashcards,e as notes,t as questions};